Q: Passing exceptions across a C API boundary A: <p>Some environments support this more or less directly.</p>
<p>For instance, if you enable <a href="http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/windows/desktop/ms680657(v=vs.85).aspx" rel="nofollow noreferrer">structured exception handling</a> and C++ exceptions through the <a href="http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/1deeycx5(VS.71).aspx" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>/EH</code></a> compiler switch, you can have C++ exceptions implemented over Microsoft's structured exception handling (&quot;exceptions&quot; for C).  Provided these options are set when compiling all your code (the C++ at each end and the C in the middle) stack unwinding will &quot;work&quot;.</p>
<p><strong>However, this is almost always a Bad Idea.</strong>  Consider that the piece of C code in the middle is:</p>
<pre><code>WaitForSingleObject(mutex, ...);
invoke_cxx_callback(...);
ReleaseMutex(mutex);
</code></pre>
<p>And that the <code>invoke_cxx_callback()</code> invokes your C++ code that throws an exception.  You will leak a mutex lock.  Ouch.</p>
<p>You see, the thing is that most C code is not written to handle C++-style stack unwinding at any moment in a function's execution.  Moreover, it lacks destructors, so it doesn't have <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resource_Acquisition_Is_Initialization" rel="nofollow noreferrer">RAII</a> to protect itself from exceptions.</p>
<p><a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/9252210/313063">kennytm</a> has a solution for C++11 and Boost-based projects.  <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/9252216/313063">xxbbcc</a> has a more general, albeit more tedious solution for the general case.</p>

Q: SEGFAULT occuring in C in raycaster due to incorrect high values being input into map array A: <p>So after stepping through with a debugger, printf statements, comparing to my javascript port of this program I finally figured out what at least was causing the ray to not even correctly hit the horizontal line and I suspect was the cause of the segfault somehow:</p>
<pre><code>while(dof &lt; 8)
        {

            mx=(int)(rx)&gt;&gt;6;
            my=(int)(ry)&gt;&gt;6;
            mp=my*mapX+mx;

            if(mp&gt;0 &amp;&amp; mp&lt;mx*my &amp;&amp; map[mp]==1)
            {dof=8;}//Hit wall
            else
            {
                rx+=xo;
                ry+=yo;
                dof+=1;
            }//next ray
        }
</code></pre>
<p>The line <code>if(mp&gt;0 &amp;&amp; mp&lt;mx*my &amp;&amp; map[mp]==1)</code> is checking if the mp value (where the ray hit occurred, which is assumed here as a ray dead left or right wouldn't reach here) is less than mx*my which is incorrect as those are the bit shifted components of the ray hit.</p>
<p>What should actually be occurring is that the mp value should be checked to be less than mapX*mapY which are the two variables controlling the map extents in both axes. I know that you could test something like <code>!(mp&gt;mapX*mapY)</code> as well but given how C appears to evaluate expressions in if statements, this is unnecessary.</p>
<p>I knew from the beginning it was going to be something like this, not some &quot;formatting&quot; issue, as the video I linked in my question shows the program working fine and I even got it working in javascript previously. First comment came closest to what the issue was but I didn't clock it until I carefully went through the original video and related github then noticed the text change when I copy pasted the pictured line.</p>
<p>I don't know why this mistyping resulted in a segfault though. Assuming that mp gets stupidly high you would think it still attempts to access map[] out of bounds. I guess the way C (or maybe general programming) does conditionals means that the instant a part of the condition is false it fails out of the statement?</p>
